@attribute [Route(pageUrl)]
@layout DemosMainLayout

<PageMetaTags PageUrl="@pageUrl" Title="@metaTitle" Description="@metaDescription" ImageUrl="@imageUrl" />

<PageHero Title="@pageTitle">
    <SubTitleTemplate>
        @((MarkupString)pageDescription)
    </SubTitleTemplate>
</PageHero>

<DocsLink Href="@DemoRouteConstants.Docs_RadarChart" />

<Prerequisites PageUrl="@pageUrl" />

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="How it works" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="how-it-works">
    <Block>
        The <strong>Radar Chart</strong> component visualizes multivariate data with each variable represented on axes starting from the same point, making it ideal for comparing multiple groups across several categories.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
        <ol>
            <li>Add the <code>RadarChart</code> component to your page and set its dimensions (e.g., <code>Width="600"</code>).</li>
            <li>Prepare your chart data by specifying <code>Labels</code> for each axis and one or more <code>Datasets</code> with values and styling options.</li>
            <li>Configure chart options such as <code>Responsive</code> using the <code>RadarChartOptions</code> object.</li>
            <li>Initialize the chart in <code>OnAfterRenderAsync</code> by calling <code>radarChart.InitializeAsync(chartData, radarChartOptions)</code>.</li>
            <li>Use the demo's interactive buttons to randomize data, add datasets, or add new data points dynamically and see the chart update in real time.</li>
            <li>Refer to the demo code below for practical examples of data preparation, dynamic updates, and chart customization.</li>
        </ol>
        </div>
        This demo demonstrates how to set up a Radar Chart, bind your data, and interactively update the chart to fit your application's needs.
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(RadarChart_Demo_01_Examples)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Click event" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="click-event">
    <Block>
        Set the <code>OnClick</code> callback to react when a radar data point is selected. The callback receives <code>ChartClickEventArgs</code> with the dataset, point index, label, and raw value; the demo displays the selected item below the chart.
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(RadarChart_Demo_02_Click_Event)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Export image" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="export-image">
    <Block>
        Export the chart as an image with <code>ToBase64ImageAsync</code>, which returns a base64 data URL you can show, store or send, or save it straight to a file in the browser with <code>DownloadImageAsync</code>.
        If the chart is still animating, both wait for the animation to finish, and the image is streamed from the browser, so Blazor Server needs no larger message size.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Keep a reference to the chart with <code>@@ref</code> and initialize it.</li>
                <li>Call <code>ToBase64ImageAsync()</code> for a PNG data URL, or pass <code>ChartImageType.Jpeg</code> and a quality from 0 to 1.</li>
                <li>Call <code>DownloadImageAsync("radar-chart.png")</code> to save the image as a file without sending it to .NET.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(RadarChart_Demo_03_Export_Image)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Show and hide datasets" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="show-and-hide-datasets">
    <Block>
        Show and hide whole datasets or single points from C#, check whether a dataset is visible and count the visible datasets.
        Visibility changed at runtime, from C# or a legend click, stays in place when <code>UpdateAsync</code> refreshes the data, because datasets are matched by <code>Key</code>, or else by label, and points by label.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Call <code>HideAsync(datasetIndex)</code> and <code>ShowAsync(datasetIndex)</code>, or <code>SetDatasetVisibilityAsync(datasetIndex, visible)</code>.</li>
                <li>Pass a data index too, for example <code>HideAsync(0, 2)</code>, to hide one point. This hides the point's marker only; the outline still passes through its value.</li>
                <li>Read the state with <code>IsDatasetVisibleAsync</code> and <code>GetVisibleDatasetCountAsync</code>.</li>
                <li>Refresh with <code>UpdateAsync(data, options, ChartUpdateMode.None)</code> to apply new data without animation; hidden datasets and points stay hidden.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(RadarChart_Demo_04_Show_Hide_Datasets)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Legend click event" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="legend-click-event">
    <Block>
        Set <code>OnLegendClick</code> to react when a legend item is clicked. The callback receives <code>ChartLegendClickEventArgs</code> with the dataset index, the legend text and whether the dataset is hidden after the click.
        Set <code>LegendClickToggles="false"</code> to stop the legend from hiding datasets, for example to open details instead.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Add an <code>OnLegendClick</code> callback to the chart.</li>
                <li>Keep <code>LegendClickToggles</code> at <code>true</code> for the default hide and show, or set it to <code>false</code> to handle the click yourself.</li>
                <li>Use <code>DatasetIndex</code>, <code>Text</code> and <code>Hidden</code> from the event arguments.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(RadarChart_Demo_05_Legend_Click_Event)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Hover event" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="hover-event">
    <Block>
        Set <code>OnHover</code> to react when the pointer moves onto a different point. The callback receives <code>ChartHoverEventArgs</code> with the same item details as <code>OnClick</code>.
        It is called only when the hovered point changes, and once with a <code>null</code> <code>DatasetIndex</code> when the pointer leaves all points, so it does not flood Blazor Server with calls.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Add an <code>OnHover</code> callback to the chart.</li>
                <li>Check <code>DatasetIndex</code>: a value means a point is hovered; <code>null</code> means the pointer left all points.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(RadarChart_Demo_06_Hover_Event)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Programmatic highlight" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="programmatic-highlight">
    <Block>
        Highlight points and show their tooltip from C# with <code>SetActiveElementsAsync</code>, for example to point at a value from a table next to the chart.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Pass one or more <code>ChartActiveElement(datasetIndex, index)</code> items to <code>SetActiveElementsAsync</code>.</li>
                <li>Pass an empty list to clear the highlight and the tooltip.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(RadarChart_Demo_07_Programmatic_Highlight)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

<Section Class="p-0" Size="HeadingSize.H4" Name="Update modes and animation control" PageUrl="@pageUrl" Link="update-modes-and-animation-control">
    <Block>
        Choose how <code>UpdateAsync</code> animates with <code>ChartUpdateMode</code>, and control the running animation with <code>StopAsync</code>, <code>RenderAsync</code>, <code>ResetAsync</code> and <code>ClearAsync</code>.
        These map one-to-one to the Chart.js <code>update(mode)</code>, <code>stop()</code>, <code>render()</code>, <code>reset()</code> and <code>clear()</code> methods.
        <br /><br />
        <strong>How to use:</strong>
        <div class="content">
            <ol>
                <li>Call <code>UpdateAsync(data, options)</code> for the default animated update, or pass <code>ChartUpdateMode.None</code> to apply the change at once. An animated update moves each matched dataset from its current values to the new ones, and a new dataset grows in. Datasets are matched by <code>Key</code> when set, otherwise by <code>Label</code>; datasets that share a label, or have none, pair in order.</li>
                <li>Call <code>StopAsync()</code> to end any running animation; the chart stays as it was drawn at that moment. Call <code>UpdateAsync</code> again to animate to new values.</li>
                <li>Call <code>ResetAsync()</code> to put the chart back in its starting state, before its first animation, and <code>RenderAsync()</code> to draw that state. Stop any running animation first, or its next frame draws over the reset. Call <code>ResetAsync()</code> and then <code>UpdateAsync(data, options)</code> to regrow the chart from its starting state, as on first render.</li>
                <li>Call <code>ClearAsync()</code> to clear the canvas; <code>RenderAsync()</code> draws the chart again. A running animation draws over the cleared canvas on its next frame, so stop it first or wait for it to end.</li>
            </ol>
        </div>
    </Block>
    <Demo Type="typeof(RadarChart_Demo_08_Update_Modes)" Tabs="true" />
</Section>

@code {
    private const string pageUrl = DemoRouteConstants.Demos_RadarChart;
    private const string pageTitle = "Radar Chart";
    private const string pageDescription = "Explore interactive <code>Blazor Radar Chart</code> examples with source code. Learn how to visualize categorical data, customize chart appearance, and enhance your Blazor applications using the Radar Chart component. Try live demos and discover practical usage scenarios.";
    private const string metaTitle = "Blazor Radar Chart Examples & Interactive Demos";
    private const string metaDescription = "Explore interactive Blazor Radar Chart examples with source code. Learn how to visualize categorical data, customize chart appearance, and enhance your Blazor applications using the Radar Chart component. Try live demos and discover practical usage scenarios.";
    private const string imageUrl = DemoImageSrcConstants.RadarChart;
}
